🚽 Test case: round-23 review finding #1 -- evaluate_multi_array_access()
🚽 (ast.c) dispatched a pointer-typed array's (`rizz *ptrs[N]`) element
🚽 address purely on the array's BASE VarType (sizeof(int) stride here),
🚽 while get_type_size_for_descriptor() had already allocated each
🚽 element as sizeof(uintptr_t) (round 21's own struct-field fix
🚽 established this exact "pointer_level dominates representation"
🚽 rule; the array-storage side of it was never applied here). Element 0
🚽 happens to start at byte 0 under EITHER stride, masking the bug
🚽 completely -- this fixture deliberately exercises element 1 and
🚽 beyond, where the strides diverge: under the old (wrong) int stride,
🚽 `ptrs[1]` would land 4 bytes into `ptrs[0]`'s own 8-byte slot instead
🚽 of at its real 8-byte-aligned address, corrupting BOTH logical
🚽 pointers on write. Confirmed via a temporary A/B test (git stash to
🚽 the pre-fix commit) that this exact program crashed under ASan with a
🚽 misaligned store followed by a SEGV.
skibidi main {
    rizz x = 1;
    rizz y = 2;
    rizz *ptrs[2];
    ptrs[0] = &x;
    ptrs[1] = &y;
    yappin("%d %d\n", *ptrs[0], *ptrs[1]);
    *ptrs[1] = 99;
    yappin("%d %d\n", x, y);
    bussin 0;
}
